/* [skill, passage, the question, the right choice, two wrong choices, why] */
const ROWS=[
 ['paragraph','mallard1','Which claim is best supported by the words “great care was taken to break to her as gently as possible”?','Others treat Mrs. Mallard as fragile and fear the news could harm her.',['Mrs. Mallard is eager to hear the news.','The news is about a wedding.'],'Gentle breaking of news shows that those around her think she may not bear it well; the words say nothing about eagerness or a wedding.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'In a literary analysis paragraph, what is the job of the claim?','It states the point about the text that the paragraph will prove.',['It retells every event in order.','It gives the writer’s feelings about the author.'],'The claim is the arguable point. Evidence and commentary then prove it.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'What is commentary in a body paragraph?','Your explanation of how the evidence proves the claim.',['A second quotation placed right after the first.','A summary of the plot so far.'],'Commentary is the writer’s own thinking: it connects the quoted words to the claim and says why they prove it.'],
 ['paragraph','lamp','Which sentence is commentary, not evidence or a claim?','The word “still” shows that she keeps hoping even after hours of waiting.',['“The lamp was still lit in the window.”','She is a hopeful person.'],'The first choice explains what a word in the text shows; the quotation is evidence, and the last choice is a claim.'],
 ['paragraph','ozyBoth','Which thesis statement is arguable and about the text, not a plain fact or a summary?','Shelley uses the ruined statue to argue that the power of rulers does not last.',['Shelley’s “Ozymandias” was first published in 1818.','“Ozymandias” is a poem about a statue in a desert.'],'A thesis takes a position that others could test against the text. The publication date is a fact and the last choice only summarizes.'],
 ['paragraph','heart','Which sentence introduces the quotation most smoothly?','The narrator protests his sanity from the start, asking, “why will you say that I am mad?”',['“Why will you say that I am mad?” is a quotation.','The narrator, a quotation is below.'],'A good lead-in names who speaks and what the words do, then the quotation fits into the sentence.'],
 ['paragraph','mallard2','Which commentary best explains how the quotation supports the claim that Mrs. Mallard feels sudden relief?','The exclamation points and the repeated word “free” show a rush of feeling she cannot hold back.',['The quotation is short, so it cannot support a claim.','The quotation uses a word that appears in many stories.'],'Strong commentary names specific features of the words, such as punctuation and repetition, and ties them to the claim.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'A body paragraph in literary analysis usually begins with what?','A topic sentence that states the paragraph’s claim.',['A long quotation with no explanation.','A summary of the whole story.'],'The topic sentence tells the reader what the paragraph will prove.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'What is the best use of an ellipsis (...) inside a quotation?','To show that you left out words from the middle without changing the meaning.',['To add your own opinion inside the quotation.','To show that the quotation comes from another author.'],'An ellipsis marks omitted words. You may not use it to change what the author means.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'A student thesis reads: “The story is about a woman who hears bad news.” Why is it weak?','It only summarizes and makes no arguable point about how the story works.',['It uses too many quotations.','It states an opinion about the author’s life.'],'A thesis must say something about the text that needs proof. A plot summary does not.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'Which tense is standard when you describe what happens in a work of literature?','Present tense, as in “Shelley shows.”',['Future tense, as in “Shelley will show.”','Past perfect tense, as in “Shelley had shown.”'],'The literary present treats a text as alive on the page each time it is read.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'Which sentence opens a second body paragraph that adds a further, supporting point to the first?','In addition, the sound of the lines reinforces this idea.',['However, the sound of the lines reinforces this idea.','In conclusion, the sound of the lines reinforces this idea.'],'“In addition” adds a point. “However” signals contrast, and “In conclusion” ends the essay.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'What should the conclusion of a literary analysis do?','Show what the argument as a whole reveals, without only repeating the introduction.',['Introduce a brand-new claim with new evidence.','Apologize for any weak evidence.'],'A conclusion leaves the reader with the significance of the argument. New claims belong in body paragraphs.'],
 ['paragraph','ozyBoth','Which detail is the strongest evidence for the claim that the poem shows how little pride can last?','The boast “Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!” set against “Nothing beside remains.”',['The words “King of Kings” taken alone.','The total number of lines in the poem.'],'Strong evidence sets two details against each other: the boast, then the empty sands that mock it.'],
 ['paragraph',null,'How much evidence does a strong body paragraph need?','Enough specific evidence to prove the claim, with each piece explained.',['One quotation, never explained.','As many quotations as will fit, with no explanation.'],'Quantity is not the test. Evidence must be specific, sufficient and followed by commentary.'],
 ['compare','pairHT','Which statement names a theme that both texts share?','A person’s will can stay unbroken against hardship.',['Wealth brings lasting happiness.','Home is the safest place to be.'],'Text A thanks the gods for an unconquerable soul, and Text B ends on not yielding. Both celebrate an unbroken will.'],
 ['compare','pairHT','Which difference between the texts is accurate?','Text A gives thanks for a soul that cannot be conquered, while Text B names actions: to strive, to seek and to find.',['Text A is about a sea voyage, while Text B is about a prison.','Text A is a play, while Text B is a letter.'],'Both are poems. Text A states a quality of the soul; Text B lists verbs of action.'],
 ['compare',null,'Which transition word introduces a similarity between two texts?','Likewise',['However','In contrast'],'“Likewise” points to a similarity; “However” and “In contrast” point to differences.'],
 ['compare',null,'Which transition word introduces a difference between two texts?','Whereas',['Likewise','Similarly'],'“Whereas” sets one thing against another, while “likewise” and “similarly” point to likeness.'],
 ['compare','pairHT','Which comparative thesis is strongest?','Although both texts champion an unbroken will, “Invictus” does so in defiant statements about the self, while “Ulysses” does so in a call to keep moving.',['Both texts are poems and both are old.','Text A is better because it is shorter.'],'A comparative thesis names the shared idea and the difference in how each author develops it.'],
 ['compare','pairOz','How do the two texts differ in what they suggest about a forgotten ruler?','Text A shows a boast with nothing left, while Text B shows people still honoring the statue.',['Text A shows people honoring the statue, while Text B shows a boast with nothing left.','Both show crowds cheering the ruler.'],'In Text A the boast is mocked by the empty sands; in Text B villagers still bring flowers to the statue.'],
 ['compare','pairOz','What theme do both texts share?','A ruler’s name and fame may be forgotten over time.',['Rulers should always build statues.','Flowers grow best in deserts.'],'In both texts a statue outlasts the memory of the person it shows.'],
 ['compare','pairOz','How does the tone of Text A compare with the tone of Text B?','Text A is ironic and bleak, while Text B is gentle and warm.',['Text A is warm, while Text B is bitter.','Both are cheerful.'],'The ruined boast in Text A is bleakly ironic; the flowers in Text B are a warm, quiet act.'],
 ['compare',null,'When you compare two texts on a shared theme, what should you compare?','How each author’s choices, such as images, structure and tone, develop the theme.',['Only which text is longer.','Only which author is more famous.'],'A comparison looks at craft: how each text builds the shared idea differently.'],
 ['compare',null,'In a block organization of a comparison essay, what is the order?','All the points about Text A, then all the points about Text B.',['One point at a time, switching between the texts.','The conclusion first, then the evidence.'],'Block organization treats one text fully before the other; point-by-point alternates between them.'],
 ['compare',null,'Why do writers often compare two texts that treat the same theme differently?','To show how different choices shape a similar message.',['To prove one text copied the other.','To avoid quoting either text.'],'Comparison shows what each author’s choices add to a shared idea.'],
 ['compare','pairHT','In Text A, what does the image of night “black as the pit from pole to pole” suggest about the speaker’s situation?','It is dark and hard, though the speaker still holds on to his soul.',['It is bright and easy.','It is a place for celebration.'],'The darkness stands for suffering, and the next lines set the speaker’s unconquerable soul against it.'],
 ['compare',null,'Which pair of texts is best suited to a comparison of a shared theme?','A poem and a short story that both show pride leading to a fall.',['A poem and a recipe that share only a rhyme.','Two stories chosen because they have the same number of pages.'],'A shared theme gives a comparison something to prove; page counts and rhymes do not.'],
 ['compare','pairFox','What theme do both passages share?','People sometimes belittle what they cannot get.',['Patience is always rewarded.','Prizes should be shared equally.'],'The fox calls the grapes sour and the boy calls the prize worthless after failing to win.'],
 ['compare','pairFox','How do the two passages differ?','Text A uses an animal and food, while Text B uses a person and a prize.',['Text A is a poem, while Text B is a play.','Text A has no conflict, while Text B has no ending.'],'The theme is shared, but the characters and objects differ.'],
 ['character','mallard1','What does the care taken to break the news “as gently as possible” reveal about how others see Mrs. Mallard?','They see her as delicate and in need of protection.',['They see her as cruel to her friends.','They see her as indifferent to news.'],'Gentle handling suggests that others think she is fragile.'],
 ['character','mallard2','What does the exclamation “Free! Body and soul free!” show about Mrs. Mallard’s inner life?','She feels an intense sense of release.',['She feels bored.','She feels nothing at all.'],'The exclamation points and repetition convey a powerful feeling, which makes her more complex than a woman who simply grieves.'],
 ['character','tomas','What do Tomas’s loud laughter and his evening alone show about him?','He hides worry behind a cheerful front.',['He truly has no worries.','He dislikes jokes.'],'His public mood and private act do not match, which is how a complex character is revealed.'],
 ['character','tomas','Which detail is the best evidence that Tomas is anxious about money?','He sat in the dark shop and counted the same few coins again.',['He laughed loudly at every joke.','He told everyone the bakery was doing fine.'],'Counting the coins again is a private act of worry; the public acts show the front he puts on.'],
 ['character','ilse','What change does Ilse go through, and what shows it?','She moves from guarding her telescope to sharing it, shown by lending her good lens.',['She moves from sharing her telescope to guarding it, shown by locking it away.','She does not change, since she owns a telescope throughout.'],'The passage contrasts refusing the boy with teaching him and lending him her lens.'],
 ['character','ilse','How might Ilse’s change advance a theme?','It supports the theme that generosity can replace possessiveness.',['It proves that telescopes are expensive.','It shows that the new boy is lazy.'],'A character’s change is how a story shows its message at work in a life.'],
 ['character','garron','What makes Captain Garron a complex character?','He has two strong wishes that conflict.',['He wants nothing.','He never changes his mind.'],'Safety and the record cannot both be won, so he is pulled two ways.'],
 ['character','garron','Which detail best shows Garron’s inner conflict?','He ordered the sails reefed, then ordered them let out.',['The passage names a captain.','The passage mentions a crew.'],'The reversed orders show a mind going back and forth between two goals.'],
 ['character','garron','What does Garron’s long pause at the rail suggest?','He is weighing two goals he cannot both reach.',['He has forgotten his orders.','He is bored by the crossing.'],'The pause follows the reversed orders and fits a character torn between two wishes.'],
 ['character','meera','How do Meera’s actions at the beginning and the end together advance the theme?','Hiding the vase and then confessing show a movement from avoiding a mistake to owning it.',['They show that she is clumsy.','They show that vases are fragile.'],'The two actions are opposites, and the change between them carries the theme.'],
 ['character','meera','Which statement of theme do Meera’s choices best support?','Taking responsibility for a mistake is a sign of growth.',['Curtains are good hiding places.','Mothers should never be told the truth.'],'A theme is a full statement of the message the character’s change carries.'],
 ['character','ishmael','What does the narrator’s “Call me Ishmael” show about how he presents himself?','He invites you to use that name, which suggests he is choosing how to present himself.',['He is certain that no one has ever heard his name.','He is writing a letter to his mother.'],'The phrase is an invitation rather than a flat statement of fact, so it hints that the narrator shapes his own image.'],
 ['character',null,'What is a character’s motivation?','The reason behind a character’s choices.',['The place where a story happens.','The order of events.'],'Motivation explains why a character acts, and strong analysis ties actions to motives.'],
 ['character',null,'How does a character’s change usually advance a theme?','The change shows the story’s message at work in a life.',['It replaces the plot.','It makes the setting more important.'],'Readers see a theme most clearly in what a character learns, loses or decides.'],
 ['character','bram','What does Bram’s habit of aiming wide when the deer looks at him suggest?','His sympathy conflicts with his skill as a hunter.',['He cannot see well.','He wants the deer to attack him.'],'A skilled shot who misses on purpose is torn between ability and feeling.'],
 ['diction','heart','What effect does the repetition of “nervous” and the phrase “very, very dreadfully” have on the narrator’s voice?','It makes him sound agitated and over-insistent.',['It makes him sound calm and bored.','It makes him sound funny and relaxed.'],'Piling up intensifiers creates a voice that protests too much.'],
 ['diction','heart','What does the punctuation, with its dashes and exclamation point, most create?','A breathless, interrupted rhythm.',['A slow, steady rhythm.','A silent pause.'],'The dashes break the sentence into gasps that suit a nervous speaker.'],
 ['diction','heart','Why does the question “why will you say that I am mad?” make a reader doubt the narrator?','He is defending himself against a charge before anyone has proved it.',['He admits in plain words that he is mad.','He never mentions madness.'],'A speaker who argues so hard for his sanity makes readers wonder.'],
 ['diction','ozyBoth','The words “colossal” and “Wreck” appear together. What effect do they have?','Great size beside ruin, which stresses how far the king has fallen.',['Joy at a new building.','A calm, ordinary description.'],'Pairing enormous with wrecked makes the contrast between past power and present ruin.'],
 ['diction','ozyBoth','What mood do “boundless and bare” and “lone and level sands” create together?','Emptiness and desolation.',['Crowded excitement.','Warm comfort.'],'Every word in the closing lines stresses vast, empty space.'],
 ['diction','ozyBoth','The boast “Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!” becomes ironic because of what?','The only works left are a wreck and empty sand.',['The works are still being built.','The boast is spoken in a whisper.'],'Irony is the gap between what the king expected and what the reader sees.'],
 ['diction','ozyBoth','What is the cumulative effect of the repeated b and l sounds in “boundless and bare” and “lone and level”?','A slow, flat, hollow music that matches the empty landscape.',['A fast, comic bounce.','A sudden shout.'],'Sound and sense work together; the soft repeated sounds feel flat and empty.'],
 ['diction','othello','What does the phrase “green-eyed monster” make jealousy seem like?','A living creature that feeds on its victim.',['A harmless pet.','A kind of food.'],'The image gives jealousy a body and a hunger, a metaphor that is also personification.'],
 ['diction','othello','What connotation does “monster” carry that the word “feeling” would not?','Fear and danger.',['Comfort and calm.','Humor and play.'],'Connotation is the emotional color a word adds beyond its meaning.'],
 ['diction','ulyEnd','What effect does the string of short verbs “to strive, to seek, to find” create?','A determined, driving rhythm.',['A hesitant, uncertain rhythm.','A rhythm of rest and surrender.'],'The brisk repeated verbs build forward momentum.'],
 ['diction','ulyEnd','Why does ending on “not to yield” give the line force?','It sets will against defeat, so the line ends on defiance.',['It is longer, so it is automatically stronger.','It repeats the first verb.'],'The last words refuse surrender, leaving defiance as the final impression.'],
 ['diction','saggy','What does the simile “sagged … like a tired animal” suggest about the house?','It is worn out and drooping.',['It is brand new and strong.','It is full of noisy visitors.'],'The simile gives the house a tired body, a quiet picture of age.'],
 ['diction','trudge','How does the verb “trudged” change the sentence compared with “walked”?','It suggests heavy, weary steps.',['It suggests dancing.','It suggests no movement at all.'],'“Trudged” carries the connotation of effort and tiredness.'],
 ['diction','smile','What tone do “thin smile” and “counting the minutes” give the sentence?','Strained impatience.',['Delighted surprise.','Calm contentment.'],'A thin smile is held back, and counting minutes shows waiting that is hard to bear.'],
 ['diction',null,'Which question best helps you analyze a writer’s diction?','Why did the writer choose this word instead of a similar one?',['How many letters are in the word?','Is the word in the dictionary?'],'Diction analysis compares the chosen word with the alternatives it excludes.'],
 ['diction','heart','How does the word “dreadfully” add to the narrator’s tone?','It adds fear and distress to his nervousness.',['It adds cheerfulness.','It adds boredom.'],'“Dreadfully” carries the connotation of dread, so the nervousness sounds extreme.'],
 ['structure','mallardPair','What does the first sentence lead a reader to expect?','That the news of the death will be dangerous to her health.',['That she will laugh at once.','That she will leave for a journey.'],'The mention of a heart trouble and the gentle breaking of news set up fear for her health.'],
 ['structure','mallardPair','How does the last sentence create surprise?','The reported cause of death, “joy that kills,” reverses the grief the opening led a reader to expect.',['It repeats the opening word for word.','It introduces a new narrator.'],'The ending turns the expected grief into the phrase “joy that kills.”'],
 ['structure','mallardPair','How does the ending echo the beginning?','Both sentences concern her heart, but the meaning has changed.',['Both sentences describe a garden.','Neither sentence mentions her heart.'],'The same heart is a weakness at the start and part of an ironic twist at the end.'],
 ['structure','mallardPair','The story covers only about an hour. What is the effect of compressing time like this?','It intensifies emotion by keeping events close together.',['It makes the story seem slow and unemotional.','It removes all conflict.'],'A short span packs a sequence of strong feelings into a tight space.'],
 ['structure','ozy0','The poem is told as the speaker repeating what a traveller said. What is the effect of this nested telling?','It puts distance between the reader and the king, so the story reaches us secondhand across time.',['It shows the king speaking directly to us.','It proves the story is false.'],'The speaker retells a traveller’s report, which stresses how far the king is from the present.'],
 ['structure','ozyBoth','Where in these lines does the poem turn from the king’s boast to the ruin?','At “Nothing beside remains.”',['At “King of Kings.”','At “My name.”'],'The sentence “Nothing beside remains” cuts off the boast and starts the picture of ruin.'],
 ['structure','hana','What is the effect of the sentence beginning “Three years earlier”?','It explains why the bread matters to her and deepens the waiting.',['It ends the story.','It introduces a new character with no link to Hana.'],'The flashback adds a memory that gives the present waiting more weight.'],
 ['structure','pavel','How does the structure create suspense and then surprise?','It withholds what is in the room, then reveals something small and unexpected.',['It tells the ending first.','It repeats the same sentence.'],'The locked door builds questions, and the wooden horse answers them with an unexpected, gentle image.'],
 ['structure','hall','What effect does the long run-on sentence followed by the fragment “Then, a hand.” have?','It stretches the waiting, then jolts the reader.',['It speeds the action throughout.','It removes all tension.'],'The long sentence delays, and the short fragment strikes suddenly.'],
 ['structure','reverse','What does telling a story in reverse order allow an author to do?','Reveal the ending first so the reader sees the past with knowledge of how it ends.',['Hide the ending until the last page.','Skip the middle of the story.'],'Starting at the funeral colors the wedding and the first meeting with what the reader already knows.'],
 ['structure','wen','What effect does the repeated pattern before the last clause create?','A routine that makes the missing sheep a shock.',['A list of unrelated facts.','A conclusion with no conflict.'],'Repetition sets a pattern, and the break in the last line stands out.'],
 ['structure','letter','What does ending the story on the blank page do?','It leaves a question open for the reader to think about.',['It ties up every detail.','It proves the letter was a lie.'],'An open ending invites readers to supply their own meaning.'],
 ['structure',null,'In a story’s structure, what is a turning point?','A moment when the direction of the story or a character’s understanding changes.',['The first sentence of the story.','The title of the story.'],'A turning point shifts the story, and the shift often drives the theme.'],
 ['structure',null,'Why might an author interrupt an action scene with a quiet memory?','To slow the pace and add emotional context.',['To make the scene shorter.','To remove a character.'],'A pause in the action slows the rhythm and deepens the stakes.'],
 ['structure',null,'When a story reveals its ending first, the reader’s interest shifts mainly from what happens to what?','How and why it happened.',['Where the author was born.','Who published the book.'],'Once the ending is known, suspense moves to how events led there.'],
 ['world','quenra','What does Linh’s action show about the custom in Quenra?','Hospitality is a duty, and refusing the cup can seem unfriendly.',['Tea is cheap in Quenra.','Guests are expected to leave early.'],'The passage says a refusal is thought to refuse friendship, so Linh drinks even when full.'],
 ['world','saying','What can a proverb in a story tell a reader about a culture?','It shows values the community passes along.',['It proves the story is true.','It shows the author’s birthday.'],'Proverbs carry shared wisdom and what a community prizes.'],
 ['world',null,'Why might a translated work lose or change some meaning?','Words, sounds and idioms in one language may have no exact match in another.',['Translators never read the original.','All languages use the same words.'],'Translation always involves choices because languages do not map word for word.'],
 ['world',null,'Many scholars think epics such as Beowulf were first told aloud before they were written down. Which feature fits that oral origin?','Repeated phrases that help a speaker remember and an audience follow.',['Footnotes at the bottom of each page.','A table of contents.'],'Formulaic repeated phrases are a typical aid for spoken performance.'],
 ['world',null,'Chinua Achebe’s novel Things Fall Apart (1958) is set in an Igbo community in what is now Nigeria. What does that setting let the novel show?','Life, customs and the arrival of colonial rule seen from inside the community.',['Only a tourist’s quick view.','A story set in medieval England.'],'A setting inside the community lets the book present its own people’s point of view.'],
 ['world',null,'The Ramayana is an ancient Sanskrit epic of India that has also been retold in many other languages and arts. What does that long history suggest?','Its story and values have been adapted by many communities.',['It has been forgotten.','It was never read aloud.'],'Wide retelling shows a story that different communities have reshaped to fit their own world.'],
 ['world','yuki','What does this moment show?','Two cultures greet differently, and each person is acting from custom.',['The elder dislikes Yuki.','Yuki has forgotten how to bow.'],'A bow and a handshake are customs. The passage shows both sides acting politely in different ways.'],
 ['world','amani','What does the narrator’s statement reveal about the culture?','It values stories over a count of years.',['It cannot count.','It dislikes old men.'],'Counting stories instead of years shows what the narrator’s culture honors in a person.'],
 ['world',null,'Which approach is the most responsible way to read a work from a culture not your own?','Read for what the work shows, using evidence, and avoid assuming that one story speaks for a whole culture.',['Assume the author speaks for everyone in the culture.','Skip any custom you do not understand.'],'Careful readers rely on the text and stay aware that cultures are varied.'],
 ['world',null,'A reader finds a custom in a novel strange. What is the best response?','Look in the text for why the custom matters to the characters.',['Decide the custom is foolish.','Ignore the scene.'],'Understanding a custom’s meaning inside the story is the first step to interpreting it.'],
 ['world',null,'What is cultural context?','The beliefs, customs and history that shape a work and its characters.',['The font used on the cover.','The number of pages in a book.'],'Cultural context explains the world a text comes from.'],
 ['world',null,'Why does the setting of a story from another culture matter to its theme?','Setting shapes what characters value and what choices are open to them.',['It never affects characters.','It only decides the weather.'],'Place and community limit and guide a character’s options, so they shape the message.'],
 ['world',null,'Which of these is an idiom that could be hard to translate word for word?','It’s raining cats and dogs.',['The cat is on the mat.','Two plus two is four.'],'An idiom’s meaning is not the sum of its words, so it often needs a different expression in another language.'],
 ['world',null,'In a story, a narrator from one culture calls another culture’s customs “backward” without explaining them. What does this suggest?','The narrator may be biased.',['The narrator is a neutral observer.','The customs are certainly harmful.'],'A judgment with no explanation shows the narrator’s own assumptions, not facts.'],
 ['world','lantern','What does the silence during the ceremony suggest about the community’s values?','Respect and remembrance of the dead.',['Dislike of festivals.','Fear of the river.'],'Silence until the last light has gone marks the occasion as solemn and focused on those who have died.'],
 ['source','ulyDrink','Tennyson’s speaker is the Greek hero Ulysses (Odysseus), back home on Ithaca after his long journey, but restless. What has Tennyson done with the source?','He starts from the hero’s homecoming and imagines a restless wish to set out again.',['He copies the Odyssey line by line.','He turns Ulysses into a farmer.'],'The poem takes the hero known from the Odyssey and imagines what follows, which is a new meaning.'],
 ['source','ulyDrink','Which words show the hero’s refusal to settle?','“I cannot rest from travel”',['The title of the poem.','“I met a traveller.”'],'The first line says directly that he cannot rest.'],
 ['source',null,'Tennyson’s “Ulysses” also owes something to Dante’s Inferno (Canto 26, about 1320), where Ulysses sails beyond the known world. Why might a poet draw on more than one source?','To combine ideas and create a new character with a new meaning.',['Because only one source is allowed.','To avoid having a theme.'],'Authors often blend sources and add their own ideas.'],
 ['source',null,'Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein (1818) carries the subtitle “The Modern Prometheus.” What does the subtitle do?','It alludes to the Greek myth of a figure who defied the gods to give humans fire, linking Victor to that story.',['It says the novel is a history of Greece.','It names the book’s printer.'],'An allusion borrows a myth’s meaning for the new work.'],
 ['source',null,'Shakespeare drew on Plutarch’s Lives, in Thomas North’s English translation, for Julius Caesar and other Roman plays. What does a writer usually do with a source like this?','Select, reshape and add speeches and emphasis to fit the new work.',['Copy it exactly.','Replace it with a blank page.'],'A source supplies material that the author reshapes for a new purpose.'],
 ['source',null,'Jean Rhys’s novel Wide Sargasso Sea (1966) tells the earlier life of a character who is a silent figure in Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre (1847). What does retelling from a different character’s point of view do?','It brings out a perspective the source left silent.',['It erases the source.','It proves the source was wrong about history.'],'A shift in point of view can show what the source passed over.'],
 ['source','pitcher','What stays the same and what changes between the source and the new version?','The idea of clever persistence stays; the crow becomes a girl and the setting a drought.',['Nothing stays the same.','Only the title changes.'],'Both versions drop objects into a container to raise water. The characters and setting change.'],
 ['source',null,'Which choice shows an author transforming a source rather than copying it?','Setting an old story in a new time and giving it a different ending.',['Copying every line and signing a new name.','Quoting the source and adding nothing.'],'Transformation means changing the source in a way that creates new meaning.'],
 ['source',null,'When a modern novel is described as a “reimagining” of a myth, what does this mean?','It keeps key elements of the myth but changes details to express new ideas.',['It is a word-for-word copy of the myth.','It has nothing to do with the myth.'],'A reimagining stays recognizably close to the source while remaking it.'],
 ['source','wolf','Which change most shifts the meaning of the old tale?','Making the wolf a lonely neighbor, which turns a tale of danger into a story about misjudging someone.',['Keeping the wolf dangerous in every way.','Changing the color of the pot.'],'A change in character changes the theme.'],
 ['source',null,'What should an analysis of a transformed source compare?','What the author keeps, what the author changes, and what the change means.',['Only the page counts.','Only the titles.'],'The analysis weighs the changes against the source to find the new meaning.'],
 ['source',null,'An allusion differs from a retelling because an allusion is what?','A brief reference, while a retelling rebuilds the whole story.',['A longer story with a new title.','A plot summary.'],'An allusion is a short echo, while a retelling reworks the whole story.'],
 ['source','tower','How does the new version change the princess’s role?','She becomes an active agent instead of someone who waits.',['She becomes the villain.','She disappears.'],'Picking the lock changes a passive role into an active one.'],
 ['source',null,'Which term names the older work that a new work is based on?','Source text',['Footnote','Epilogue'],'The older work an author draws on is the source text.'],
 ['source',null,'Hamlet is thought to draw on an older Scandinavian tale of a prince named Amleth, recorded by Saxo Grammaticus about 1200. What is gained by comparing the two?','You can see what Shakespeare added or changed to deepen the main character.',['You can prove Shakespeare never existed.','You can ignore the play.'],'The comparison shows the author’s choices and the new meaning they make.'],
 ['source',null,'Which pair is a source and its transformation?','Homer’s Odyssey and Tennyson’s “Ulysses”',['Tennyson’s “Ulysses” and Shelley’s “Ozymandias”','Shelley’s “Ozymandias” and a cooking blog'],'Tennyson’s poem takes a hero from Homer and gives him a new situation.']];
/* The Try-it questions in pool form; the generator skips them. */
const TRY=['A student writes three sentences about a fictional passage in which “The lantern swung from Nia’s hand all the way home, though the moon was full.” Sentence 1: “Nia is afraid of the dark.” Sentence 2: “The lantern swung from Nia’s hand all the way home, though the moon was full.” Sentence 3: “Carrying a light under a full moon shows that her fear does not depend on what she can see.” Which sentence is the commentary? A: Sentence 1, B: Sentence 2, C: Sentence 3. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional passage: “The letter arrived on Monday. Elio read it, folded it, and put it in the drawer. Years later, the drawer was empty, and he still checked it every morning.” Which structural choice shows time passing? A: A flashback, B: A jump forward in time, C: A frame story. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional passages: Text A, “The knight rode out to fight the dragon and came home a hero.” Text B, “The dragon, tired of being hunted, left the cave to find a quieter hill.” What does Text B do to its source? A: It copies Text A exactly, B: It retells the story from the dragon’s point of view, C: It leaves the dragon out. Answer A, B or C.'];
const tryIt=s=>`Try it: ${s[0].toLowerCase()+s.slice(1)}`;
export default {
id:'language-h-english-10-literary-analysis',subject:'language',level:5,grade:9,standard:'CCSS ELA Reading: Literature, RL.9-10.1 to 9-10.6 and 9-10.9',
title:'Literary Analysis',
goal:'Write literary analysis that argues from the text: build a claim, evidence and commentary across paragraphs, compare two texts on a shared theme, explain how complex characters develop and advance a theme, judge the cumulative effect of word choices, trace how structure and time create tension and surprise, read world literature in its cultural context, and show how an author transforms a source.',
points:20,
skills:{paragraph:'Claim, evidence and commentary',compare:'Comparing two texts',character:'Complex characters and theme',diction:'Word choice and tone',structure:'Structure, time and surprise',world:'World literature and culture',source:'Transforming a source'},
outline:[['RL.9-10.1','Cite strong and thorough evidence in a literary analysis',['paragraph']],['RL.9-10.2','Compare how two texts develop a shared theme',['compare']],['RL.9-10.3','Analyze how complex characters develop and advance the theme',['character']],['RL.9-10.4','Analyze the cumulative impact of word choices on meaning and tone',['diction']],['RL.9-10.5','Analyze how structure and time create tension and surprise',['structure']],['RL.9-10.6','Analyze a cultural experience reflected in world literature',['world']],['RL.9-10.9','Analyze how an author draws on and transforms source material',['source']]],
steps:[
 {skill:'paragraph',say:'Literary analysis is an argument about a text, built in three moves. The claim is your arguable point. The evidence is the exact words that prove it. The commentary is your own explanation of how those words prove the claim. Most weak paragraphs have a claim and a quotation but no commentary, so the reader is left to guess the link.',pic:board('Claim, evidence, commentary',['Claim: Kofi is determined.','Evidence: “He climbed the hill again.”','Commentary: “again” shows he persists','Check: does it explain the link?'],['arguable point','the exact words','your explanation','a self-check']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[0]),answer:'C',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Commentary explains what the quoted words show. Which sentence is neither the claim nor the quotation itself?',pic:pass('Fictional student sentences',['1. Nia is afraid of the dark.','2. The lantern swung from Nia’s hand all the way home, though the moon was full.','3. Carrying a light under a full moon shows that her fear does not depend on what she can see.'])}},
 {skill:'paragraph',say:'Analysis is not one paragraph but a chain. The introduction gives context and a thesis, the main claim of the whole essay. Each body paragraph proves one part of the thesis with a topic sentence, evidence and commentary, linked by transitions. The conclusion shows what the argument reveals as a whole instead of repeating the introduction or adding new claims.',pic:flow('Anatomy of an analytical essay',[['Introduction','Context and a thesis'],['Body paragraphs','Claim, evidence, commentary'],['Conclusion','What the argument shows']])},
 {skill:'compare',say:'To compare two texts, begin with a shared theme, a message that both develop. Then look at how each author builds it. Two texts can share a theme but treat it differently through images, tone, structure or point of view. In a strong comparison, the shared theme is the claim and the differences in craft are the evidence.',pic:board('One theme, two texts',['Text A: “I am the master of my fate”','Text B (fiction): “He trimmed his sails.”','Shared theme: facing hardship','Difference: A declares, B acts'],['famous verse','fictional line','the claim','craft'])},
 {skill:'compare',say:'A comparison essay can be organized in two ways. In block organization you discuss all of Text A, then all of Text B. In point-by-point organization you take one idea at a time, such as imagery, then tone, and treat both texts under each idea. Use transitions that signal likeness, such as likewise, and difference, such as whereas.',pic:sketch('Shared theme and separate choices',[['Text A',[[0,6],[30,6],[30,28],[0,28]],0],['Shared theme',[[35,6],[65,6],[65,28],[35,28]],2],['Text B',[[70,6],[100,6],[100,28],[70,28]],1]],[[15,3,'its own craft'],[50,3,'the claim'],[85,3,'its own craft']])},
 {skill:'character',say:'A complex character wants something, is pulled by competing motives and often changes. The author shows this through choices, speech, private acts and how others respond. Ask three questions: what does this character want, what stands in the way, and what does the character do about it? The answer to the third often carries the theme.',pic:flow('How a character carries a theme',[['Wants','A goal and a fear'],['Conflict','Two pulls at once'],['Choice','Action that shows the theme']])},
 {skill:'character',say:'Complexity often appears when what a character shows in public differs from what the character does in private. The gap lets readers infer hidden feelings. To analyze it, name the public act, name the private act, state the inference, and then say how the contrast develops the theme. Always rest the inference on specific details from the text.',pic:board('Public face, private act',['Public: Dara jokes with every customer.','Private: she rereads her rejection letter at night.','Inference: she hides disappointment','Theme link: hidden feelings need a voice'],['what others see','what she does alone','the inference','the theme link'])},
 {skill:'diction',say:'Diction is an author’s choice of words, and its cumulative impact is what many words add up to. One strong word may create an image, but a pattern of words builds tone and mood. In a famous sonnet the speaker describes the ruined statue with the phrase below. Notice how the size and the damage pull in opposite directions.',pic:pass('Shelley’s sonnet, 1818',['Two vast and trunkless legs of stone','Stand in the desert.','Cumulative effect: size beside ruin, a lonely emptiness.'],false)},
 {skill:'diction',say:'Analyze diction by comparing the word chosen with the alternatives. Walked is neutral, trudged is heavy and weary, strode is confident. Each word carries a connotation, an emotional color beyond its dictionary meaning. Quote the key words, name the connotation, and then say what tone or mood the whole pattern builds.',pic:board('Choosing the exact word',['Walked: neutral','Trudged: heavy, weary','Strode: confident','Ask: why this word instead of a similar one?'],['plain movement','tired steps','sure steps','the question for analysis'])},
 {skill:'structure',say:'Structure is the order and shape of a text, and it controls what a reader expects. An author can set up an expectation, complicate it, and then reverse it. A surprise ending lands hardest when the opening has led the reader to expect the opposite. When you analyze structure, name the choice and explain its effect on the reader.',pic:flow('A surprise ending in three moves',[['Set up','Opening creates an expectation'],['Complicate','Middle adds a hidden feeling'],['Reverse','Ending overturns the expectation']])},
 {skill:'structure',say:'Time is a tool too. A flashback jumps to an earlier moment to explain the present. A jump forward skips ahead to show change. Reverse order places the ending first so the reader asks how events led there. Pacing depends on context: short sentences can quicken action or isolate a moment for emphasis; longer sentences can slow reflection or carry a rush of actions. Consider the words and punctuation as well as length.',pic:board('Time on the page',['Flashback: jumps to an earlier moment','Jump forward: skips ahead','Reverse order: ending first','Pacing: length, punctuation and context'],['explains the present','shows change','shifts interest to how','can change tension']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[1]),answer:'B',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'The passage says “Years later.” Does it go back to an earlier moment or skip ahead?',pic:pass('Fictional passage',['The letter arrived on Monday. Elio read it, folded it, and put it in the drawer. Years later, the drawer was empty, and he still checked it every morning.'])}},
 {skill:'world',say:'World literature comes from many languages, places and times, and it reflects cultural experience: customs, beliefs and daily life. To read it fairly, notice what a custom shows about what a community values, and look for the reason inside the text. Avoid treating one story as speaking for a whole culture, and remember that a translation is itself a set of choices.',pic:pass('Fictional passage',['At the harvest table in Tessel, the youngest serves the oldest first, and no one eats until the grandmother lifts her bowl.','Ask: what does the custom show about what this community values?'])},
 {skill:'world',say:'Works of world literature come from many places and eras. The Old English poem Beowulf survives in a manuscript from about the year 1000, Miguel de Cervantes published the first part of Don Quixote in Spain in 1605, Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein was first published in 1818, and Chinua Achebe published Things Fall Apart in 1958. Reading across time and place shows both what cultures share and what they do not.',pic:{t:'timeline',events:['about 1000: Beowulf manuscript','1605: Don Quixote, Part 1','1818: Frankenstein published','1958: Things Fall Apart']}},
 {skill:'source',say:'Authors draw on sources: myths, older stories, histories and other books. To transform a source is to keep some elements and change others to create new meaning. Compare what the author keeps, what the author changes, and what the change means. A different ending, setting or narrator can reverse the message of the old story.',pic:flow('How a source is transformed',[['Source','The older story'],['Choices','Keep, change, add'],['New meaning','What the change shows']]),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[2]),answer:'B',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Text B tells about the dragon’s wish and feelings. Whose point of view is that?',pic:pass('Fictional passages',['Text A: The knight rode out to fight the dragon and came home a hero.','Text B: The dragon, tired of being hunted, left the cave to find a quieter hill.'])}},
 {skill:'source',say:'Homer’s Odyssey, a Greek epic, follows Odysseus home to Ithaca after the Trojan War. Alfred Tennyson wrote “Ulysses” in 1833 and first published it in 1842. Using the hero’s Latin name, he imagines him at home but restless, wanting to sail again. The poet keeps the hero and changes the situation, inviting readers to consider his desire to keep seeking.',pic:tab('A source and its transformation',[['Homer, Odyssey','The hero struggles to return home to Ithaca'],['Tennyson, “Ulysses” (published 1842)','At home, the aged hero longs to sail again']],['What each shows'])},
 {skill:'paragraph',summary:true,say:'Tie the unit together with one habit: argue from the text. State an arguable claim, quote the exact words, and write commentary that explains how they prove it. Then widen the lens: compare two texts on a shared theme, trace a character’s change, weigh word choices, structure and time, read world literature in context, and compare a work with its source.',pic:flow('Literary analysis: the habit',[['Claim','One arguable point'],['Evidence','The exact words'],['Commentary','How they prove it']])}],
progression:{requires:['language-h-english-9-reading-literature','language-m-claims-evidence'],operations:2,demand:'Write literary analysis that argues from the text: claim, evidence and commentary, a comparison of two texts on a theme, complex characters, the cumulative effect of word choices, structure and time, world literature in context, and an author’s transformation of a source.'},
items(add){
 const seen=new Set(TRY);
 const put=(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept)=>{if(seen.has(prompt))return;seen.add(prompt);add(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept);};
 ROWS.forEach(([skill,key,q,right,wrong,why],k)=>{const c=choose(right,wrong,k);
  if(!key){put(skill,`${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,null/* 25 Oct: no picture; it only repeated the question in tiny type */,why,[right,dot(right)]);return;}
  const [src,lines,verse]=P(key),text=lines.join(verse?' / ':' ');
  put(skill,`${src}: “${text}” ${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,pass(src,lines),why,[right,dot(right)]);});
}};
